\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Four-phase time-division multiplexing. A single $A_{31}$ multiplier instance is time-shared across the four real/imaginary cross-products of one complex multiply; no second multiplier is instantiated, which is what keeps the DSP48E1 count at 64 rather than 256.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tdm}{{1}{3}{Four-phase time-division multiplexing. A single $A_{31}$ multiplier instance is time-shared across the four real/imaginary cross-products of one complex multiply; no second multiplier is instantiated, which is what keeps the DSP48E1 count at 64 rather than 256}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Row-major schedule of the $3\times 3$ multiply. Each result element accumulates its three $k$-terms and is emitted ($\downarrow $) as soon as it completes, so the result stream is part of the compute schedule rather than a separate drain stage. The $\approx $224-cycle latency is 216 cycles of arithmetic plus load and commit overhead --- there is no reorder buffer and no output queue.}}{3}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fsm}{{2}{3}{Row-major schedule of the $3\times 3$ multiply. Each result element accumulates its three $k$-terms and is emitted ($\downarrow $) as soon as it completes, so the result stream is part of the compute schedule rather than a separate drain stage. The $\approx $224-cycle latency is 216 cycles of arithmetic plus load and commit overhead --- there is no reorder buffer and no output queue}{figure.2}{}}
